---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import PersonaCard from '../components/PersonaCard.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Compare from '../components/Compare.astro';
import RooreCta from '../components/RooreCta.astro';
import { ROORE_PAGES } from '../lib/roore';

const principles = [
  { id: 'personas', label: 'Specialized roles' },
  { id: 'anti-jobs', label: 'Clear Don\'ts' },
  { id: 'trigger', label: 'On-demand vs routines' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Avoid over-asking' },
  { id: 'approvals', label: 'Approvals' },
  { id: 'one-job', label: 'One bot, one job' },
  { id: 'state', label: 'State on disk' },
  { id: 'usage', label: 'Keep usage down' },
  { id: 'security', label: 'Shared computer' },
  { id: 'pitfalls', label: 'Common pitfalls' },
  { id: 'iterate', label: 'Start small' },
];

const burns = [
  'Long, chatty CoS threads (the full history is resent)',
  'A catch-all "General Helper"',
  'Inbox triage against years of backlog on day one',
  'Always-on routines',
  'Browser clicks where a plugin would do',
  'Event listeners like "every new Slack message"',
];

const saves = [
  'One job per Bot',
  'New conversations per task',
  'Schedules during office hours',
  'Plugin before browser',
  'Grok Bot as the outer loop (research, drafts, delegation); heavy code jobs in Cursor',
  '"If nothing: send nothing" in every scheduled Bot',
  'Check Settings → Usage & Billing before large jobs',
];

const pitfalls = [
  ['Multiple Bots ≠ isolation', 'One computer, all logins. Do not put a password on the computer that another Bot should not be able to use.'],
  ['X credits run out quietly', 'A Bot watching X may report "nothing new" instead of an error.'],
  ['Site layout changes', 'A scraper can report an empty scan for weeks and sound calm. Ask it to report its inputs, not only findings.'],
  ['Hiding a Bot does not pause routines', 'Hide only removes it from the sidebar. Pause routines separately.'],
  ['Delete leaves traces', 'Delete removes profile, conversation, and routines, but files and logins on the shared computer may remain.'],
  ['Older blogs lie about platforms', 'Linux and Android exist now. iPad still does not.'],
  ['Crypto-desk posts', 'Threads promising thousands a week lack verifiable receipts. Treat as ads.'],
];
---

<Layout
  lang="en"
  title="Grok Bot best practices – approvals, usage, security"
  description="Ten principles for Grok Bots that work: specialized roles, Don'ts, Require Approval, one bot per job, state on disk, and keeping the weekly quota down."
  datePublished="2026-09-05"
  dateModified="2026-09-10"
  breadcrumb="Best practices"
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Best practices · 10 principles"
    title="How to set up bots that actually work"
    lede="From official docs and what builders on X repeat. Most of it is about writing down the boundaries."
  />

  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-5 sm:px-8 py-12 grid lg:grid-cols-[13rem_1fr] gap-12">
    <nav aria-label="Principles" class="hidden lg:block">
      <ul class="sticky top-24 space-y-1 text-sm">
        {
          principles.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a href={`#${p.id}`} class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-1.5 text-muted hover:text-ink transition-colors">
                <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-cobalt shrink-0 translate-y-[-1px]" aria-hidden="true" />
                <span>{p.label}</span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose-guide max-w-2xl">
      <section id="personas">
        <h2 class="!mt-0">Tight personas, not fluffy descriptions</h2>
        <p>
          The persona should be specific enough that the bot knows exactly what to do, and what it
          <strong>must not</strong> do.
        </p>
        <Compare
          bad="You are a helpful assistant that helps me with everything I need."
          badWhy="Too broad. The bot does not know what it is good at."
          good="You handle my inbox and do prioritization. Every morning you read the latest 20 emails, categorize them by priority (Urgent/Normal/Can wait), and list them in a markdown document. You NEVER reply to email for me."
          goodWhy="Clear scope. The bot knows its job and its limits."
        />
      </section>

      <section id="anti-jobs">
        <h2>Define Don'ts clearly</h2>
        <p>
          Don'ts are as important as jobs. They protect you from unexpected "helpful" actions, the bot that sends the
          email it was only supposed to draft.
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose my-6">
          <PersonaCard
            lang="en"
            name="Sales Bot"
            role="Follows up leads and keeps the CRM updated."
            jobs={[
              'Follow up leads that have not replied within 3 days',
              'Update the CRM with the latest interaction',
              'Suggest next steps based on the lead\'s reply',
            ]}
            antiJobs={[
              'NEVER send email without my approval',
              'NEVER delete leads from the CRM',
              'NEVER offer discounts or special pricing',
              'Make NO decisions that affect pricing',
            ]}
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="trigger">
        <h2>On-demand vs routines</h2>
        <p>Choose the trigger method based on the task:</p>
        <div class="not-prose grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
          <div class="bg-panel border border-line rounded-lg p-5">
            <p class="font-mono text-[0.7rem] px-2 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-line-soft text-ink-soft inline-block mb-3">On-demand</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-2">Manual trigger. Use for:</p>
            <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-[0.95rem]">
              <li>Tasks that need fresh context ("analyze this competitor's site")</li>
              <li>Things you want to double-check before they run</li>
              <li>Workflows you are still testing</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-panel border border-line rounded-lg p-5">
            <p class="font-mono text-[0.7rem] px-2 py-0.5 rounded-md bg-cobalt-soft text-cobalt inline-block mb-3">Routine</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted mb-2">Scheduled. Use for:</p>
            <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-[0.95rem]">
              <li>Repetitive tasks that should always happen (morning inbox triage)</li>
              <li>Reports generated regularly (weekly sales summary)</li>
              <li>Tasks where "forgetting" is worse than "running once too often"</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <Callout kind="tip" title="Tip">
          <p>
            The order: <strong>once → make it reliable → save as skill → test → only then schedule</strong>. A routine
            inherits every unspoken assumption in the task it was built from. A wrong morning routine can run four days
            before you notice. Always <strong>Test run</strong> before it goes live.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      <section id="questions">
        <h2>Avoid over-asking</h2>
        <p>A common mistake: ask the bot for confirmation constantly. That kills the point of automation.</p>
        <Compare
          badLabel="Over-asking"
          bad="Run prioritization on the mail in my inbox. Ask me before you categorize each email. Also ask whether to include mail from the spam folder."
          badWhy="The bot cannot work independently. You end up answering a pile of questions."
          goodLabel="Clear rules"
          good="Run prioritization on the mail in my inbox. Ignore the spam folder. Rules: mail from customers = Urgent, newsletters = Can wait, everything else = Normal."
          goodWhy="The bot knows the rules and works without asking."
        />
        <Callout kind="warn" title="When the bot should ask">
          <p>
            If the task can have large consequences (emailing customers, deleting files, handling money), let the bot
            ask before it acts. That is the difference between over-asking and a fence.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      <section id="approvals">
        <h2>Approvals: two layers</h2>
        <p>
          Don'ts in the description are layer one. Layer two is the product's own rules under
          <strong>Settings → General → Auto-review</strong>. Set <strong>Require Approval</strong> for:
        </p>
        <ul class="not-prose flex flex-wrap gap-2 my-5">
          {
            ['send', 'publish', 'buy', 'delete', 'change production'].map((a) => (
              <li class="font-mono text-[0.8rem] px-3 py-1.5 rounded-md bg-panel border border-signal/40 text-signal">{a}</li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Require Approval</strong> wins over <strong>Always Allow</strong>.</p>
        <h3>Least privilege</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Connect only the tools the flow needs</li>
          <li>Start read-only and drafts</li>
          <li>Service accounts where the source system allows it</li>
          <li>Review plugins and active routines regularly</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Grok Bot can shop online via @link: you approve each purchase; the Bot gets a one-time card. At time of
          writing: USA, rolling out to mobile.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="one-job">
        <h2>One bot, one job</h2>
        <p>Resist the urge to build a superbot that does everything. It gets messy fast.</p>
        <h3>Why specialists work better</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Easier to debug:</strong> when something goes wrong, you know which bot it is</li>
          <li><strong>Simpler personas:</strong> less context means clearer instructions</li>
          <li><strong>Scales:</strong> you can run several bots in parallel</li>
          <li><strong>Faster iteration:</strong> change one bot's persona without affecting others</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Example fleet</h3>
        <div class="not-prose bg-panel border border-line rounded-lg divide-y divide-line-soft my-5">
          {
            [
              ['Chief of Staff', 'Pinned. The only one you talk to. Delegates.'],
              ['Inbox / calendar', 'Handles only mail and meetings'],
              ['Research Bot', 'Finds data and summarizes'],
              ['One narrow specialist', 'For your actual job'],
            ].map(([name, job]) => (
              <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-4 gap-y-0.5 px-4 py-3">
                <span class="font-display font-semibold w-36">{name}</span>
                <span class="text-[0.95rem] text-ink-soft">{job}</span>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <p>
          Tell CoS before you create several. Use group chat when the handoff itself should be visible. Duplicate a Bot
          when the same role needs a new scope (the copy gets profile, skills, and routines, but not history or
          memory).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="state">
        <h2>State on disk, not in the chat</h2>
        <p>
          Docs: <code>/workspace</code> on the cloud computer survives computer updates. Builders on X:
          <em>"Chat is history. That file is memory."</em>
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>One Bot writes the board file; the others read</li>
          <li>Two Bots writing the same file can lose an edit with no error</li>
          <li>New conversation per task; let the file carry the context</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="usage">
        <h2>Keep usage down</h2>
        <p>
          The most documented complaint: the weekly quota runs out faster than people expect. @bot reset quotas for
          everyone on 5 Sep 2026, and did the same on 26 Aug when access widened. Do not count on that every week.
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
          <div class="bg-panel border border-line rounded-lg p-5">
            <p class="eyebrow text-signal mb-3">What burns</p>
            <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-[0.95rem]">
              {burns.map((b) => <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="mt-[0.55rem] shrink-0 w-3 h-[2px] bg-signal" aria-hidden="true" /><span>{b}</span></li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-panel border border-cobalt/40 rounded-lg p-5">
            <p class="eyebrow text-cobalt mb-3">What holds</p>
            <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-[0.95rem]">
              {saves.map((s) => <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="mt-2 shrink-0 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-cobalt" aria-hidden="true" /><span>{s}</span></li>)}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="security">
        <h2>All Bots share one computer</h2>
        <p>
          The cloud computer is one per account, not per Bot. Logins, cookies, and files are shared. Each Bot gets its
          own screen so they can work in parallel, but <strong>screens are not a security boundary</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Never paste passwords or one-time codes into the chat. Take over Agent Computer and type yourself.</li>
          <li>Plugins are account-wide: one login applies to all Bots.</li>
          <li>Hide a Bot if you might need it. Delete removes profile, conversation, and routines, not files and sessions on the computer.</li>
          <li>Do not show secrets in a Teach a task recording.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="pitfalls">
        <h2>Common pitfalls</h2>
        <div class="not-prose bg-panel border border-line rounded-lg divide-y divide-line-soft my-5">
          {
            pitfalls.map(([title, body]) => (
              <div class="px-4 py-3">
                <p class="font-display font-semibold">{title}</p>
                <p class="text-[0.95rem] text-ink-soft">{body}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="iterate">
        <h2>Start small, iterate fast</h2>
        <p>Build the team week by week:</p>
        <ol class="not-prose !pl-0 space-y-2 my-5">
          {
            [
              ['Week 1', 'Create a Chief of Staff, pin it, write fences. One five-part task against a document, then one in a real tool.'],
              ['Week 2', 'Find a task you do manually at least 3 times a week. Run it, make it reliable, save as a skill.'],
              ['Week 3', 'Test run, then a weekday-morning routine. Keep write actions behind approval.'],
              ['Week 4', 'Add a specialist for what you still do manually. Ask CoS first.'],
            ].map(([when, what]) => (
              <li class="flex items-baseline gap-4 bg-panel border border-line rounded-lg px-4 py-3">
                <span class="font-mono text-[0.75rem] text-cobalt whitespace-nowrap w-16">{when}</span>
                <span class="text-[0.95rem]">{what}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <Callout kind="ok" title="After a month">
          <p>
            One bot running prioritization, 2–3 specialists, and enough experience to know which tasks can be
            automated.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-16 bg-ink text-white rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8">
        <p class="eyebrow text-white/60 mb-3">Summary</p>
        <ul class="!pl-0 space-y-2 !text-white/90 list-none">
          <li>Write tight personas with clear jobs <em>and</em> Don'ts</li>
          <li>Once → reliable → skill → test run → routine</li>
          <li>Require Approval for send, publish, buy, delete, change production</li>
          <li>One bot per job, state in a file, new conversations per task</li>
          <li>Office hours, plugin before browser, "if nothing: send nothing"</li>
          <li>All Bots share one computer. Screens are not a security boundary</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-5 sm:px-8 pt-8">
    <RooreCta
      lang="en"
      heading="'Don'ts' is exactly how we build for companies"
      body="Deterministic where the answer should be the same every time. Prepared material and your yes where it must not proceed automatically. If you have an invoice, order, or email flow done by hand every week, we can build that first."
      source="grokbot.se best practices"
      href={ROORE_PAGES.aiMedarbetare}
    />
  </div>
</Layout>
